EXT. SALT FLAT STAGE STATION - NOON

White ground, white sky. A lone shack. A dead well. Heat shimmer.

SILAS MORROW (50s, dusty duster, gray stubble) sits on a bench with a rifle across his knees. CATALINA VEGA (30s, wide hat, twin pistols) stands in the road ten paces away.

CATALINA
You're a long way from Tucson, Silas.

SILAS
I was told you'd be here.

CATALINA
By who?

SILAS
By a man who owed me.

CATALINA
He owed you what?

SILAS
A reason.

She smiles. Steps forward. He doesn't move.

CATALINA
You killed my brother.

SILAS
I did.

CATALINA
Why?

SILAS
He drew first.

CATALINA
He was nineteen.

SILAS
So was I, once.

The wind stops. Both of them listen.

CATALINA
Stand up.

SILAS
My knees won't allow it.

She draws one pistol. He lifts the rifle. They both freeze, muzzles level.

CATALINA
Last words?

SILAS
Tell the boy's mother I'm sorry.

A gunshot. Both flinch. Both dive for cover.

On the ridge, a distant rider tumbles from a horse. The two lie in the salt, breathing. They look at each other.
